    explanation = 'Option A (correct) For Amazon RDS, AWS takes responsibility for the underlying operating system, including patching, which shifts this task from the customer (who would manage the OS on an EC2 instance) to AWS as part of the managed service offering. Option B (incorrect) While the customer is indeed responsible for database engine patching when running a database on Amazon EC2, this option describes the customer''s responsibility for EC2, rather than highlighting a shift where AWS takes on responsibility for RDS. Option C (correct) AWS manages the database engine updates and patching for Amazon RDS, relieving the customer of this operational burden and representing a significant shift from the customer''s responsibility to manage the database engine when deployed on Amazon EC2. Option D (incorrect) AWS is always responsible for the physical infrastructure, including physical server maintenance, for both Amazon EC2 and Amazon RDS as part of the "security of the cloud" model. Option E (incorrect) Application-level security, such as managing database users, permissions, and securing the application connecting to RDS, remains the customer''s responsibility as part of "security in the cloud."'
