    explanation = 'Option A (incorrect) An Internet Gateway enables communication between instances in a VPC and the internet, but it does not facilitate direct connections between multiple VPCs or between VPCs and on-premises networks through a central hub. Option B (correct) VPC Peering allows for connecting two VPCs directly, enabling instances in either VPC to communicate with each other as if they were on the same network, which addresses the "connecting multiple VPCs together" part of the question. Option C (incorrect) A NAT Gateway enables instances in a private subnet to connect to the internet or other AWS services while preventing inbound connections from the internet, but it does not connect multiple VPCs or VPCs to on-premises networks. Option D (correct) AWS Transit Gateway acts as a central hub to connect multiple VPCs and on-premises networks, simplifying network topology and connectivity management for complex environments. Option E (incorrect) Amazon Route 53 is a highly available and scalable Domain Name System (DNS) web service that translates domain names to IP addresses, but it is not a service for establishing network connectivity or routing data plane traffic between VPCs or on-premises networks.'
