    explanation = 'Option A (correct) Replatform is the second simplest strategy because it involves modifying the application to leverage cloud-managed services, such as migrating a database to Amazon RDS or containerizing an application for Amazon ECS, requiring specific application configuration changes but not a full re-architecture. Option B (correct) Refactor is the most complex strategy because it involves re-architecting and rewriting significant parts of the application to take full advantage of cloud-native capabilities like serverless functions (AWS Lambda) or microservices (Amazon EKS/ECS), leading to extensive code changes. Option C (correct) Rehost is the simplest strategy because it involves moving applications to the cloud with minimal or no changes, typically "lift-and-shift" operations onto Amazon EC2 instances using tools like AWS Application Migration Service (MGN), which primarily focuses on infrastructure migration. Option D (correct) Repurchase is the third most complex strategy because it involves replacing an existing application with a new Software-as-a-Service (SaaS) solution (e.g., migrating from an on-premises CRM to Salesforce), which requires no changes to the old application''s code but often involves significant data migration, configuration of the new service, and integration development with existing enterprise systems, making it more complex than replatforming in terms of ecosystem integration.'
