    explanation = 'Option A (incorrect) When training a classification model on an imbalanced dataset using AWS Machine Learning services, accuracy can be misleading because a model might achieve high accuracy by simply predicting the majority class for all instances, thus failing to correctly identify the minority class, which is often the target of interest. Option B (correct) The F1 Score, being the harmonic mean of precision and recall, is highly appropriate for imbalanced datasets because it provides a balanced measure that penalizes models that perform poorly on either precision (false positives) or recall (false negatives), ensuring that models trained with AWS services like Amazon SageMaker effectively identify both classes, especially the rare one. Option C (incorrect) Root Mean Squared Error (RMSE) is a metric primarily used for regression problems to measure the average magnitude of the errors between predicted and actual values, making it entirely irrelevant for evaluating the performance of a classification model, regardless of dataset balance, within an AWS machine learning workflow. Option D (incorrect) While precision measures the proportion of positive identifications that were actually correct, focusing solely on precision for an imbalanced dataset might lead to an AWS-deployed model having high precision but low recall, meaning it correctly identifies positive cases when it does, but misses a large proportion of actual positive cases, particularly critical for rare events.'
