UPDATE public.question
SET
    explanation = 'Option A (incorrect) A random split might lead to one of the splits having too few or no instances of the rare fraudulent class, making the training unrepresentative or the evaluation unreliable, a scenario AWS SageMaker Autopilot attempts to avoid for classification by default using stratified approaches. Option B (correct) A stratified split ensures that the proportion of rare fraudulent cases is maintained across both the training and test sets, which is crucial for building robust fraud detection models and evaluating them effectively, a practice supported by AWS services like SageMaker for dataset preparation and training. Option C (incorrect) A time-series split preserves the temporal order of data but does not inherently guarantee a representative distribution of rare fraudulent events across the splits, potentially leading to an unrepresentative test set in terms of class balance, which is not its primary function in AWS machine learning workflows. Option D (incorrect) Simple random sampling for splitting the dataset does not guarantee that the rare fraudulent cases will be evenly distributed across the training and test sets, potentially resulting in an unrepresentative sample for model training or evaluation, unlike more sophisticated methods supported in AWS Glue or SageMaker for imbalanced data. Option E (correct) K-fold cross-validation, particularly stratified K-fold, ensures that each fold maintains the class distribution of the original dataset, providing multiple representative training and test sets to robustly evaluate model performance on rare fraudulent transactions across different data partitions, a strategy applicable with AWS SageMaker custom training jobs or orchestrations.'
WHERE id = 'q_14_m_14_3_137';

