    explanation = 'Option A (incorrect) Enabling S3 Transfer Acceleration utilizes AWS Edge Locations to speed up data transfers between clients and S3 buckets over long distances, but it does not improve the internal S3 service performance or reduce latency within the bucket itself for high access rates. Option B (correct) Distributing objects across multiple prefixes in an S3 bucket is a key performance optimization strategy because S3 automatically scales throughput based on the number of distinct prefixes, allowing for parallelization of requests and reducing the likelihood of hot spots that cause increased latency. Option C (incorrect) Enabling S3 Versioning protects against accidental deletions or overwrites by preserving multiple versions of an object, which is a data protection feature and does not directly enhance read/write performance or reduce latency for high-access patterns. Option D (incorrect) Reducing the storage class to S3 Standard-IA primarily affects storage costs for infrequently accessed data and, while it offers similar low latency to S3 Standard, changing the storage class does not address the fundamental issue of internal S3 request scaling needed to optimize performance for high access rates.'
