    explanation = 'Option 0 (incorrect) Storing all data in S3 Standard is not an optimal strategy for infrequently accessed data because S3 Standard is designed for frequently accessed data and has a higher per-GB storage cost compared to other S3 storage classes like S3 Standard-IA or S3 Glacier, leading to unnecessary expenses. Option 1 (incorrect) Using S3 One Zone-IA for all data is not a comprehensive optimization strategy because while it offers lower costs, it stores data in a single Availability Zone, making it vulnerable to data loss during an AZ disruption and not suitable for all data requiring high availability and durability. Option 2 (correct) Applying S3 Lifecycle policies to transition objects to lower-cost storage classes like S3 Standard-IA or S3 Glacier is the ideal strategy because it automates the movement of data to progressively cheaper S3 storage tiers based on access patterns and age, directly optimizing costs for infrequently accessed data. Option 3 (incorrect) Migrating data from S3 to Amazon EBS Cold HDD (sc1) volumes attached to EC2 instances is not an S3 cost optimization strategy and introduces additional operational overhead, EC2 instance costs, and fundamentally changes the storage paradigm from managed object storage to block storage, which is outside the scope of optimizing S3 costs.'
