    explanation = 'Option A (correct) Spot Instances allow you to bid on unused EC2 capacity and offer the deepest discounts (up to 90% off On-Demand prices) for fault-tolerant applications that can tolerate occasional interruptions, as AWS can reclaim the instance with a two-minute warning if capacity is needed elsewhere. Option B (incorrect) On-Demand Instances provide high flexibility with no long-term commitment and are billed by the second, but they offer no discounts for sustained use and are significantly more expensive than Spot Instances for interruptible workloads. Option C (incorrect) Reserved Instances offer significant discounts (up to 75% off On-Demand prices) in exchange for a 1-year or 3-year commitment, making them ideal for stable, predictable workloads, but they do not provide the deepest discounts for applications that can tolerate interruptions when compared to Spot Instances. Option D (incorrect) Dedicated Hosts provide instances on physical servers dedicated to your use, offering licensing flexibility and compliance benefits, but they are typically the most expensive EC2 purchasing option as you pay for the entire host and do not offer cost savings for interruptible workloads compared to Spot Instances.'
