    explanation = 'Option A (correct) DNS resolution is a VPC attribute that, when enabled, allows instances within the VPC to use the Amazon-provided DNS server (at the .2 IP address of the VPC''s CIDR) to resolve public domain names on the internet. Option B (incorrect) DNS hostnames is a VPC attribute that, when enabled along with DNS resolution, ensures EC2 instances with public IP addresses are assigned corresponding public DNS hostnames, but it does not enable the instance to resolve *other* public domain names. Option C (incorrect) VPC peering connects two VPCs privately, allowing resources in either VPC to communicate with each other, but it is unrelated to an EC2 instance resolving public domain names from the internet. Option D (incorrect) Private hosted zones in Route 53 are used to manage DNS records for custom domains that are resolvable only within specified VPCs, which is for internal resolution, not for enabling the VPC to resolve external public domain names.'
