    explanation = 'Option A (incorrect) While it is technically possible to upload a third-party certificate to AWS Certificate Manager (ACM) for use with CloudFront, this option does not fulfill the requirement for automatic certificate renewal, as third-party certificates typically require manual renewal and re-upload. Option B (correct) AWS Certificate Manager (ACM) is the ideal service because it allows users to provision public SSL/TLS certificates for free, automatically manages certificate renewal, and seamlessly integrates with CloudFront for secure communication over custom domain names. Option C (incorrect) Generating a self-signed certificate is not suitable for a public web application because browsers do not trust self-signed certificates, leading to security warnings for end-users, and it does not provide automatic renewal capabilities. Option D (incorrect) Amazon Route 53 is a Domain Name System (DNS) web service and is responsible for translating domain names to IP addresses, but it does not perform SSL/TLS termination or manage certificates; these functions are handled by services like CloudFront or Application Load Balancer.'
