    explanation = 'Option A (incorrect) Amazon S3 is an object storage service that can serve as a repository for backups but does not inherently provide centralized backup management capabilities or point-in-time recovery features for relational databases itself. Option B (correct) AWS Backup is a centralized, fully managed backup service that allows you to configure backup policies and automatically back up various AWS services, including Amazon RDS databases, and supports point-in-time recovery. Option C (correct) Amazon RDS automatically performs backups and supports manual snapshots for its managed relational databases, enabling point-in-time recovery to any second within the defined retention period. Option D (incorrect) Amazon DynamoDB is a NoSQL database service, not a relational database, therefore it does not fit the "relational databases" criteria of the question, although it does offer its own point-in-time recovery feature. Option E (incorrect) Amazon EBS provides block storage volumes and snapshots, which can be part of a backup strategy for databases running on EC2, but it does not offer centralized backup management or direct point-in-time recovery specifically at the relational database level.'
