    explanation = 'Option A (incorrect) Spread Placement Group distributes instances across distinct underlying hardware to reduce correlated failures, which increases physical distance and network latency between them, directly contradicting the requirement for extremely low latency. Option B (incorrect) Partition Placement Group segments instances into logical partitions on separate racks primarily for fault tolerance and to manage large-scale distributed systems, which does not guarantee the lowest possible latency between all instances compared to co-location. Option C (correct) Cluster Placement Group groups instances closely together within a single Availability Zone on the same low-latency, high-bandwidth network fabric, which is ideal for HPC applications requiring maximum network performance between instances. Option D (incorrect) Dedicated Hosts provide dedicated physical servers primarily for licensing and compliance, but do not inherently offer an EC2 instance placement strategy optimized for extremely low latency and high network throughput between multiple EC2 instances as effectively as a Cluster Placement Group.'
