    explanation = 'Option A (incorrect) Using IAM policies to define permissions is fundamental for granting specific access to IAM entities, but it does not enforce a maximum ceiling that no entity can exceed, as another IAM policy could still grant broader permissions. Option B (correct) Applying permissions boundaries to IAM users and roles directly restricts the maximum permissions that an identity-based policy can grant, acting as a guardrail to enforce the strictest least privilege by limiting the effective permissions. Option C (incorrect) Implementing Service Control Policies (SCPs) on the root organizational unit restricts what actions AWS accounts within the organization can perform at an account level, but they do not directly set maximum permissions for individual IAM users and roles within a specific account. Option D (incorrect) While configuring AWS Organizations and applying restrictive IAM user policies is a good practice for consistent security, it relies on manually configuring each policy correctly and doesn''t inherently prevent a misconfigured or overly permissive IAM policy from being applied, which is what a permissions boundary specifically prevents.'
