    explanation = 'Option A (incorrect) Unit testing focuses on individual code components, not comprehensive application scalability under anticipated traffic, and running full load tests at this early stage would be excessively resource-intensive and delay rapid feedback for developers, contrary to agile development principles facilitated by AWS CI/CD services. Option B (incorrect) Conducting performance and load testing only in the production environment is a high-risk strategy that exposes actual users to potential performance bottlenecks and failures, leading to poor user experience and revenue loss, which contradicts AWS Well-Architected Framework''s operational excellence and reliability pillars that advocate for proactive testing in pre-production stages. Option C (correct) Performing performance and load testing before deployment to production environments allows for the identification and remediation of scalability issues and performance bottlenecks in a controlled, production-like environment, ensuring that AWS services like EC2 Auto Scaling, RDS, and Lambda concurrency are configured and behave correctly under anticipated load without impacting live users. Option D (incorrect) While continuous testing is beneficial, subjecting "every small code change" to a full, resource-intensive load test throughout the entire development lifecycle is inefficient and can significantly slow down the release cadence, as noted in the option itself, whereas AWS CI/CD pipelines are designed to balance rapid feedback with comprehensive validation at appropriate stages.'
