    explanation = 'Option A (incorrect) Checking the CPU utilization of the build environment is a performance metric and does not directly indicate whether the automated tests within CodeBuild passed or failed; a build can fail quickly or succeed slowly irrespective of its CPU usage. Option B (incorrect) Reviewing the total build duration only tells you how long the build took, not whether the test commands themselves executed successfully or encountered errors, as a build can be short due to an early failure or long for a successful, complex process. Option C (correct) Evaluating the exit code of the build command is the standard and most reliable method, as CodeBuild, like most shell environments, interprets an exit code of 0 as success and any non-zero exit code as a failure for the executed command or script. Option D (incorrect) Parsing detailed build logs for specific keywords using complex scripting and regular expressions is an unreliable and error-prone approach for determining build status compared to the standardized exit code mechanism that CodeBuild inherently uses, making it an anti-pattern in CI/CD best practices.'
