    explanation = 'Option A (incorrect) Blue/Green deployment shifts 100% of traffic from an old (Blue) environment to a new (Green) environment after the Green environment has been fully validated, not gradually routing a small percentage first, a pattern often facilitated by AWS CodeDeploy and Application Load Balancers. Option B (incorrect) All-at-once deployment immediately deploys the new version to all instances simultaneously, without any gradual traffic shifting or initial percentage-based routing, which is a high-risk strategy that lacks the controlled rollout mechanisms provided by services like AWS CodeDeploy. Option C (correct) Canary deployment precisely describes routing a small percentage of production traffic to the new version first, allowing for monitoring and validation before gradually increasing traffic, a strategy robustly supported by AWS CodeDeploy for EC2, Amazon ECS, and AWS Lambda deployments. Option D (incorrect) In-place deployment updates the existing instances directly without introducing a new parallel environment, typically updating the application version on the same servers, which is a deployment method rather than a traffic routing strategy involving percentage-based traffic shifting between old and new versions as done with services like AWS CodeDeploy.'
