    explanation = 'Option A (incorrect) Backup and restore involves creating backups of data and configurations using services like Amazon S3 and EBS snapshots, which are then restored to new infrastructure in the secondary region after a disaster, resulting in the longest recovery time. Option B (incorrect) Pilot light keeps the core services, such as a continuously replicated database using RDS Read Replicas, running in the secondary region, but the application servers are typically deployed from AMIs or scaled up from zero upon failover, not maintaining a fully functional scaled-down version of the entire environment. Option C (incorrect) Multi-site active/active runs a fully functional and scaled-out version of the application in multiple regions simultaneously, actively serving traffic from all sites, which provides the lowest RTO but is significantly more complex and expensive than maintaining a scaled-down version. Option D (correct) Warm standby maintains a scaled-down, but fully functional, version of the entire environment in a secondary region, with key components like EC2 instances and databases (e.g., RDS with cross-region replication or read replicas) continuously running, ready to be scaled up and have traffic rerouted via services like Route 53 with some manual intervention during a disaster.'
