-- question_id: q_36_m_36_4_40
-- answer_correct: True | verified_indices: [2] | confidence: 100%
UPDATE public.question
SET
    explanation = 'Option A (incorrect) In-place deployment directly updates the application on existing EC2 instances, which typically causes instances to be temporarily unavailable or to serve an incomplete application version, thus not guaranteeing zero downtime or the full availability of the old version during deployment and validation. Option B (incorrect) An all-at-once deployment, often a configuration within an In-place strategy, attempts to update all instances simultaneously, leading to a complete service outage until the new version is fully deployed and ready, which directly contradicts the zero-downtime requirement. Option C (correct) Blue/green deployment deploys the new application version to a separate set of EC2 instances (the green environment) while the old version (the blue environment) continues to serve traffic, allowing for independent validation of the new version and a controlled traffic shift only after successful testing, ensuring zero downtime and full operational status of the old version during the process. Option D (incorrect) A rolling update deployment, which is a common strategy for In-place deployments, updates instances in batches, meaning that some instances are taken out of service or updated sequentially, which does not ensure the entire old version remains fully operational and isolated during the validation of the new version.'
WHERE id = 'q_36_m_36_4_40';

-- question_id: q_36_m_36_4_44
-- answer_correct: True | verified_indices: [2] | confidence: 100%
UPDATE public.question
SET
    explanation = 'Option A (incorrect) In-place deployment directly updates the application on existing production instances, meaning any issues with the new version would immediately impact live users and a rollback would require another deployment, which does not allow for extensive testing on a separate environment or quick reversion. Option B (incorrect) A rolling update, while reducing immediate impact compared to an all-at-once update, still progressively updates the live production environment, meaning users will encounter issues as instances are updated and it does not provide a separate, isolated environment for extensive pre-traffic testing or instant rollback capabilities. Option C (correct) Blue/green deployment is the chosen strategy because it provisions an entirely new set of EC2 instances for the new application version, allowing the old version to continue serving traffic, enabling extensive testing on the new environment before shifting production traffic, and providing an immediate rollback mechanism by simply switching traffic back to the stable old environment. Option D (incorrect) All-at-once deployment updates all production instances simultaneously, which maximizes the risk of complete service disruption for all users if the new application version contains critical issues and offers no quick or clean way to revert to the previous stable version without another full deployment.'
WHERE id = 'q_36_m_36_4_44';

-- question_id: q_36_m_36_4_46
-- answer_correct: True | verified_indices: [2] | confidence: 100%
UPDATE public.question
SET
    explanation = 'Option A (incorrect) In-place deployment with a `HalfAtATime` configuration updates instances directly within the existing environment, leading to potential downtime for the instances being updated and not providing a separate, isolated environment for pre-traffic testing of the new version. Option B (incorrect) Rolling update deployment with a `OneAtATime` configuration updates instances sequentially within the live environment, meaning new versions receive immediate production traffic without prior dedicated testing in an isolated environment, and rollback to the exact previous state can be complex. Option C (correct) Blue/green deployment, leveraging a load balancer to shift traffic, creates a completely separate "green" environment for the new application version, allowing for thorough testing with no impact on the live "blue" environment, and only shifts customer traffic after successful validation, providing zero downtime and an easy rollback if tests fail. Option D (incorrect) All-at-once deployment updates all instances simultaneously, causing significant downtime during the update process and providing no dedicated environment for testing the new version before it impacts all customers.'
WHERE id = 'q_36_m_36_4_46';

-- question_id: q_36_m_36_4_52
-- answer_correct: True | verified_indices: [2] | confidence: 100%
UPDATE public.question
SET
    explanation = 'Option A (incorrect) In-place deployment updates the application directly on the existing Amazon EC2 instances, which typically involves taking instances offline temporarily or causing service interruption, failing to meet the zero downtime requirement and not provisioning new instances. Option B (incorrect) Rolling update replaces the application on a batch of existing Amazon EC2 instances at a time, which can still introduce periods of reduced capacity or minor downtime as each batch is updated and doesn''t maintain a separate, untouched old environment for immediate rollback. Option C (correct) Blue/green deployment provisions an entirely new set of Amazon EC2 instances (green environment) with the updated application, directs traffic to them, and keeps the original instances (blue environment) running and available for an immediate rollback, ensuring zero downtime and meeting all specified requirements. Option D (incorrect) All-at-once deployment updates the application on all Amazon EC2 instances simultaneously, which inevitably leads to significant downtime as all instances are unavailable during the deployment process and does not provision new instances or keep old ones for rollback.'
WHERE id = 'q_36_m_36_4_52';

-- question_id: q_36_m_36_4_54
