    explanation = 'Option A (correct) Performing GameDay exercises actively simulates disruptive events and validates the DR plan by requiring teams to react and follow established procedures, exposing operational and technical weaknesses in a realistic, controlled environment. Option B (correct) Implementing AWS Fault Injection Service experiments allows for the controlled injection of specific faults into the infrastructure, directly testing the system''s resilience and revealing how applications and underlying DR mechanisms behave under stress, thus exposing gaps. Option C (incorrect) Relying solely on AWS Backup for data recovery is insufficient for testing a full DR strategy because it addresses only data restoration, not the comprehensive application, infrastructure, and operational recovery processes required to bring services back online. Option D (incorrect) Using Amazon Route 53 latency routing is a traffic distribution mechanism based on lowest latency and does not inherently test or validate a DR failover strategy; dedicated Route 53 failover routing policies with health checks are required for DR, and even then, their configuration alone doesn''t constitute a full DR test. Option E (incorrect) Documenting the DR plan thoroughly without execution, while important for planning, fails to uncover practical flaws, procedural errors, or unexpected technical challenges that only become apparent when the plan is put into action.'
