    explanation = 'Option A (correct) Blue/Green deployment strategy ensures minimal downtime by deploying the new version to a distinct set of instances while the old version continues to serve traffic, allowing for instant rollback by simply redirecting user traffic if issues arise. Option B (incorrect) In-place deployment strategy updates the application directly on existing instances, which inherently causes some downtime on those instances during the update and complicates a quick, seamless rollback. Option C (incorrect) All-at-once deployment strategy updates all instances in the deployment group simultaneously, leading to significant application downtime and a high risk of service interruption, making quick recovery difficult. Option D (correct) Canary deployment strategy gradually shifts traffic to the new application version on a small subset of instances, minimizing the impact of potential issues and enabling a rapid rollback by diverting traffic from the canary group. Option E (incorrect) Rolling update deployment strategy updates instances in batches, which can minimize total downtime compared to all-at-once, but still introduces temporary service unavailability for updated instances and makes a complete, instant rollback more complex than Blue/Green or Canary approaches.'
